WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.960
Spider-Man: Homecoming is the Spidey reboot
fans have been waiting for and it's positively

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.809
crawling with easter eggs and connections
to the web-slinger's previous appearances

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:14.379
on the big and small screen, the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, plus references from over fifty

00:00:14.379 --> 00:00:16.780
years of Spider-Man history in the comics.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.990
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and there are so many easter eggs in Tom Holland's

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.210
first Spider-Man solo movie that, in this
video, I'm going to focus on the toughest-to-spot

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.080
and best easter eggs and references you might
have missed.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.020
I've also got some very cool Funko Pops plus
a pack of Spider-Man: Homecoming merchandise

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.290
to give away.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:38.320
For a chance to win, make sure you subscribe,
turn on your notifications, and leave me a

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.570
comment below about your favourites easter
eggs or any others you spotted!

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.760
Just before I start, quick warning there are
spoilers ahead for the movie, so take care

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.809
if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:52.510
Homecoming director Jon Watts has said he
took a lot of inspiration from the original

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:58.559
Spider-Man comics and there's one iconic issue,
Amazing Spider-Man #33, that features prominently

00:00:58.559 --> 00:00:59.750
in the movie.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:04.650
At one point in the film, Vulture uses his
suit to collapse a warehouse, trapping Spider-Man

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.740
under a ton of rubble and girders.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:10.540
The way Spider-Man gets out from under the
rubble, motivated by a reflection of himself

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:15.180
and the voice of Tony Stark, is similar to
how Peter visualises Uncle Ben in the comic

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.750
and uses it as a source of inner strength
to help him lift the rubble off himself.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.829
And eagle-eyed fans might also remember there
was an homage to this same iconic comic book

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:28.610
issue in Captain America: Civil War when Spider-Man
was weighed down by an airport walkway that

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.540
Captain America smashed on to him.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.840
Michael Keaton may not have read any Spider-Man
comics before bringing to life the classic

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.710
villain, Vulture, but the movie still has
a very cool easter egg to his comic book character.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:46.969
On the Staten Island Ferry, there's a car
with the license plate SM2-0563, in other

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:52.950
words Amazing Spider-Man issue 2, published
May 1963, a comic which featured the first

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.360
appearances of both Vulture and the Tinkerer,
who's the character who makes Vulture's tech

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.789
in the new movie.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:04.360
The Staten Island ferry event also tips its
hat to the fact that, in the comics, the birthplace

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.470
of Adrian Toomes, aka Vulture, is Staten Island.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:12.000
And although the look of Michael Keaton's
Vulture is quite different to his comic book

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.000
counterpart, the costume designers have said
they deliberately added a straggly fur collar

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.520
to his jacket as a nod to both the 'feathers
on a real vulture' and the original Vulture

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.610
design by artist Steve Ditko.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:27.860
Homecoming introduces the villainous Shocker
to the MCU and there's a subtle homage to

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.480
his yellow-diamond patterned costume in the
comics via the sleeves on the jacket worn

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.010
by the first Shocker Jackson Brice, and the
second Shocker Herman Schultz also has them.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:42.670
Donald Glover's character Aaron Davis tries
to buy some tech from Shocker and later Spider-Man

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.170
interrogates him in a car park to learn more
about what Vulture and his crew are up to.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:51.610
Spidey's suit analysis on Davis reveals his
alias is The Prowler, a nod to his comic book

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.040
alter ego.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.980
And during the interrogation, Davis mentions
he has a nephew in the neighbourhood.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:00.740
In the comics, Davis is the uncle of Miles
Morales who, like Peter Parker, was bitten

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:05.340
by a radioactive spider turning him into a
famous web-crawling superhero.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.890
This easter egg works on several levels, firstly
Sony have an animated movie for Miles Morales

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.020
in the works.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.460
Secondly, the nephew-mention suggests that
Miles Morales could also exist either in the

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.080
MCU or Sony's live-action Spider-Man universe.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.700
And finally, it's interesting because Donald
Glover and his fans campaigned for Glover

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.790
to play Spider-Man before the character's
first movie reboot, and later that led him

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:31.240
to voice Miles Morales in the Ultimate Spider-Man
TV series.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:35.670
Spider-Man: Homecoming very obviously connects
to the MCU with the opening scenes reliving

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.080
moments from Captain America: Civil War from
Peter Parker's point-of-view.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:44.310
But there's a less-obvious call-back to an
earlier MCU movie that I particularly enjoyed.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:48.130
Now, during this early fight scene with a
group of criminals breaking into some ATMs,

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:52.770
their masks very obviously evoke the Avengers,
but there's a more subtle joke and reference

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.290
you might not have noticed.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:58.400
As Spidey takes the thugs out, at one point
he makes Thor punch Hulk, which is a nice

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.830
reverse of how Hulk smashed Thor at the end
of the Battle of New York in the first Avengers

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:03.830
movie.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:07.640
By the way, given the criminals are wearing
Avengers masks, I also liked the identity

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.610
theft joke here in the background.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:14.490
When Happy Hogan is arranging the move of
Avengers tech like Tony's Hulkbuster armor

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:19.329
from Age of Ultron and the prototype of Captain
America's new shield, he also mentions something

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:24.920
we've not yet seen in the MCU, that is, Thor's
Magic Belt, the Norse name for which Happy

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.180
gives up trying to pronounce.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:31.250
The belt is also known in the comics as the
"belt of strength" or the "power belt" and

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.400
it gives the wearer incredible strength.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:38.540
I wonder whether the belt is just a fun easter
egg or something we might see in a future

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.500
movie, for example Ragnarok or Infinity War?

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:46.420
There's a call-back to the first Captain America
movie at the end of the scene on Coney Island

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.580
when the camera pans up to the Cyclone roller
coaster.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:54.220
In that First Avenger film, just before Steve
Rogers and Bucky zip-wire down to the HYDRA

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:59.160
train carrying Zola, they reminisce about
how Steve threw up on that same roller coaster

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.690
ride when they were younger.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:06.620
In Spider-Man: Homecoming, actor Kenneth Choi
returns to the MCU, this time as Principal

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:07.620
Morita.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.720
Choi previously played the role of Jim Morita,
a member of the Howling Commandos in the first

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.280
Captain America movie.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.250
And there's a clever easter egg to his previous
role with a picture of Jim Morita in the Principal's

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.430
office, suggesting the two men are related.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:24.570
By the way, this isn't the first time Marvel
has reused actors for new roles in the MCU.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:29.530
Alfre Woodard had a minor role as Miriam Sharpe
in Captain America: Civil War where she confronted

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:33.210
Tony Stark as the mother of a son who was
killed in the Battle of Sokovia.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:37.680
Then, later that same year, she had a much
larger role as the villainous Mariah Dillard

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.680
in the Netflix series Luke Cage.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:46.710
And Martin Starr who plays teacher Mr Harrington
in Spider-Man: Homecoming previously cameo-ed

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:51.120
in 2008's The Incredible Hulk as a computer
nerd, who was said to be Amadeus Cho in the

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.110
movie novelisation.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:57.639
Spider-Man: Homecoming really delves deep
into the capabilities of Tony Stark's Spider-Man

00:05:57.639 --> 00:06:02.210
suit and, after Peter and Ned hack into the
suit to remove the "training wheels" protocol,

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.870
we next discover that the suit is also equipped
with an AI voice to assist Peter with the

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.400
suit's functions.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.300
Peter calls the "suit lady" program Karen
and she's actually voiced by actress Jennifer

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:17.990
Connelly who played Marvel character Betty
Ross in director Ang Lee's 2003 Hulk movie,

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:20.570
a pre-MCU movie released by Universal.

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:25.420
But what's even more interesting and a sort
of MCU in-joke is that Jennifer Connelly is

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:30.330
married to Paul Bettany, who played J.A.R.V.I.S.,
the original voice inside Iron Man's suit,

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.170
before he became Vision in Avengers: Age of
Ultron.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:39.170
After Ned learns that Peter is the real Spider-Man,
he starts asking him what powers he has, for

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:42.139
example, whether he can summon an army of
spiders.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:46.170
This is a curious question because Spider-Man
did indeed use that threat against a villain

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.960
in issue 57 of Marvel Adventures Spider-Man.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:53.830
And when Ned suggests that Peter should go
and stand on the edge of a building and see

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:58.490
how far he can shoot his webs, it immediately
made me think of the first Sam Raimi Spider-Man

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.870
movie where Tobey Maguire's Spidey literally
does just that as he's learning about the

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.110
extent of his abilities.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:10.060
There's another fun homage to that first Sam
Raimi movie and the kiss the web-crawler shared

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.980
with Mary Jane as he hung upside-down after
rescuing her.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:17.610
During the Washington Monument rescue in this
film, there's a moment where Spider-Man is

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:22.020
hanging upside down after he's just saved
Liz from the plummeting elevator.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.510
Spidey's suit lady Karen even says, "This
is your chance Peter, kiss her", in a nice

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:30.389
call-back to his upside-down kiss with MJ
back in 2002.

00:07:30.389 --> 00:07:35.430
And earlier, en route to Washington, there
was a road sign warning about the Triskelion

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:36.430
Clean-Up.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.889
The Triskelion is the SHIELD headquarters
built on Theodore Roosevelt Island and the

00:07:40.889 --> 00:07:44.590
mention via the road sign is a link back to
the events of Captain America: The Winter

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:49.449
Soldier where the Triskelion was severely
damaged when one of the Project Insight helicarriers

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:52.780
crashed into the building in the movie's finale.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:58.030
Spider-Man: Homecoming introduces the Department
of Damage Control properly in the MCU, although

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:02.560
you might remember that the organization appeared
on a news report ticker playing in the background

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:07.350
during the very first Iron Man movie and also
got a mention in Agents of SHIELD.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:12.620
In the comics, Damage Control is an organization
initially owned by Tony Stark and Wilson Fisk

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:17.050
and founded by Anne-Marie Hoag, who's played
by Tyne Daly in Homecoming where she has a

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:19.919
very similar look to her comic book counterpart.

00:08:19.919 --> 00:08:26.110
Interestingly, Marvel announced a comedy TV
series called "Damage Control" back in 2015.

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:30.880
It's unclear whether this is still on the
cards, in particular after NBC cancelled "Powerless",

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:35.680
a comedy set in the DC universe that explored
a similar premise about how ordinary people

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.570
deal with the fallout from superhero activities.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:43.539
We learn in Homecoming that Tony Stark has
sold off Avengers Tower in Manhattan and is

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:45.750
moving upstate to the New Avengers Facility.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:50.710
And we get a quick cameo by DUM-E, one of
Tony Stark's two helper robots from the Iron

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:55.440
Man movies, last seen mangled up and being
driven away by Tony at the end of Iron Man

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:56.440
3.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.630
There's another Iron Man easter egg thanks
to the design of the T-shirt Peter wears when

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:03.790
he goes to see Tony Stark at the New Avengers
Facility.

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:08.490
It has the exact same science joke as the
T-shirt that Pepper Potts wore in Iron Man

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:13.100
3, which is especially fitting as, after Peter
leaves, Pepper emerges from the press conference

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:15.020
where she's been waiting for him.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:19.550
By the way, the new suit that Tony shows Peter
in this scene is also a nice tease for the

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:21.320
Iron Spider costume.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:26.020
Comic book artist Mark Bagley, who's known
for his work on titles including The Amazing

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:30.560
Spider-Man and Ultimate Spider-Man, gets a
shout-out via graffiti on a building in the

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.920
background while Spidey's on the phone to
Happy Hogan.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.650
Following Tony Stark and Aunt May's flirty
first meeting in Captain America: Civil War,

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:43.210
Tony continues his flirtation in Homecoming,
this time via a video Peter shoots on his

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:47.510
cell phone; though May later says she's not
a fan of 'that Tony Stark'.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:51.720
This is all a nod to Robert Downey Jr and
Marisa Tomei's real-life relationship in the

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.690
90s and also their on-screen relationship
in the rom-com "Only You".

00:09:55.690 --> 00:10:00.330
But Tony's not the only guy enamoured with
May – both the bodega owner and the waiter

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:02.640
at the Thai restaurant feel the same way.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.590
All of which is a pointed contrast to May's
previous movie and some of her comic-book

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.850
incarnations.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:13.140
Aunt May's joke about a turkey meatloaf recipe
is a little call-back to a humorous scene

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:18.070
in the first Amazing Spider-Man where Andrew
Garfield's Peter Parker arrives back home

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:22.820
ravenous and eats Aunt May's meat loaf, declaring
it's the best meat loaf ever.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:26.710
And when May and Peter go out for Thai food,
right next door to the restaurant there's

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:31.330
a little nod to Thor's homeland via the Korean
Church of Asgard.

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:35.890
The work of John Hughes was a particular inspiration
for Homecoming director Jon Watts and there

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:39.550
are several moments in the movie which reference
classic films by Hughes.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:44.120
For example, Zendaya's character Michelle
channels Ally Sheedy's outcast character Allison

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:45.450
from The Breakfast Club.

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:50.540
And, like Allison in the 1985 coming-of-age
classic, in Homecoming Michelle spends time

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.720
in detention, though apparently she doesn't
have to be there/neither of them has to be

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:54.720
there.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:58.029
And there are also a couple of homages to
the 1986 film Ferris Bueller's Day Off as

00:10:58.029 --> 00:10:59.029
well.

00:10:59.029 --> 00:11:02.820
First, there's the scene where Peter races
after Shocker through a suburban neighbourhood.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.470
Peter's run through gardens, past barbecues,
dogs and children is Jon Watts' ode to the

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:11.839
classic teen comedy by John Hughes, where
Ferris Bueller had to race home to get there

00:11:11.839 --> 00:11:13.300
before his family.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:17.680
In fact, we even see a little clip of Ferris's
own race against the clock on a big TV in

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:21.780
one of the gardens that Peter Parker rushes
through and Peter even says 'great movie'

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.940
as he runs by.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:28.550
The very final post-credits scene featuring
Captain America's humorous PSA about the virtues

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:32.890
of patience is a very meta-moment and joke
by the filmmakers to all of us in the audience

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:36.610
who've become accustomed to sitting through
the credits in any Marvel movie for the chance

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:39.370
to see a little tease to a future MCU film.

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:43.420
Captain America pokes fun at us with the scene
in a similar spirit to the fourth-wall-breaking

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:48.130
joke in the post-credits of Ferris Bueller's
Day Off, which was, of course, directly spoofed

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:49.589
in 2016's Deadpool.

00:11:49.589 --> 00:11:54.610
There was, of course, another after-credits
scene which featured the villain Mac Gargan

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:58.510
and there was a cool tease to his comic book
alter ego of the Scorpion via the scorpion

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:00.170
tattoo on his neck.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:04.420
The scene sets up a much bigger role for Gargan
in the sequel and his mention of having friends

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:08.570
on the outside who want to take out Spider-Man
could be a tease to the supervillain squad

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.040
the Sinister Six.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:13.839
Homecoming already introduced three characters
who've been members of the Sinister Six in

00:12:13.839 --> 00:12:19.040
the comics; Vulture, Scorpion and Shocker,
so, this is nicely half-way to a full Sinister

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:20.250
Six crew.

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:24.000
Sony has been hoping to make a Sinister Six
movie for a while now and it's not hard to

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.940
think that these characters could be spun
off eventually into a separate movie.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:32.420
A little reveal at the end of the movie by
Michelle's character is that her friends call

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:33.480
her "MJ".

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.370
This feels like a bit of a joke by the filmmakers
about the persistent rumours during production

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:39.950
that Zendaya was going to be playing Mary
Jane.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:45.140
It's not actually a "Mary Jane" reveal because
all she's saying is that her nickname is "MJ"

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:49.430
even though Spidey fans around the world will
know that MJ is, of course, synonymous with

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:50.529
"Mary Jane".

00:12:50.529 --> 00:12:54.540
With the filmmakers shying away, for the moment,
from using various characters from the Sam

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:59.010
Raimi trilogy and the Amazing Spider-Man movies,
it doesn't seem likely that we'll see a real

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.910
Mary Jane for a while.

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:05.610
Of course, Marvel might also try to find a
way to make Michelle into Mary Jane with a

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:12.270
trick like making "Mary Jane" her middle name
and, therefore, the reason for her MJ nickname.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:16.860
Mary Jane's classic nickname of "Tiger" for
Peter Parker gets some great visual shout-outs

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:21.070
at the high school via several Homecoming
banners that include the word "Tiger", plus

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:25.760
at one point, I noticed someone in a tiger
costume running around the school in the background.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.770
"Face it Tiger, you just hit the jackpot"
were Mary Jane's first words to Peter Parker

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:35.490
in the comics and Kirsten Dunst's MJ said
"Go get 'em, tiger" to Tobey Maguire's Peter

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.010
at the very end of Spider-Man 2.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:42.540
And to round off the very self-aware nature
of this Spider-Man movie, Homecoming even

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:47.690
integrated the theme tune from the original
60s Spider-Man TV show into the opening Marvel

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:50.450
logo intro with a composition by Michael Giacchino.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:55.630
So, what was your favourite easter egg or
moment in Spider-Man: Homecoming and were

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.220
there any other references I missed?

00:13:57.220 --> 00:14:00.709
Be sure to leave a comment about the movie
and subscribe for a chance to win either a

00:14:00.709 --> 00:14:03.940
Spider-Man Funko Pop or some cool official
merchandise.

